# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b7), edited.

~ rose
rose_somerton [smile]: Father says we must keep our boots wiped and welcome the grand folk properly. You have mud on yours, just like the men he feeds to the big tree.
* Ask what she means about the big tree -> rose_tree
* Ask what else her father says -> rose_father
* Bid her a polite good evening -> rose_leave

~ rose_tree
rose_somerton [sly]: Mother told me to hush my mouth about the roots drinking red, or the hare with the horns will scratch at my shutter.
-> END

~ rose_father
rose_somerton [neutral]: He says the Green Queen watches every hearth, and anyone who locks their door at night has something rotten in the cellar.
-> END

~ rose_leave
rose_somerton [smile]: Mind your manners on the Founder's Round, or Father will write your name in his little bark book.
-> END

~ victoria
victoria_wainwright [neutral]: Stand clear of the crates, if you please. I am minding Eddie and Zachariah while the Trading Post is busy, and I do not know your faces.
* Ask how long she has lived here -> victoria_past
* Ask about the two younger boys -> victoria_boys
* Promise not to trouble them -> victoria_leave

~ victoria_past
victoria_wainwright [weary]: Since the fog took our mother and father out by Dawn's Gate. Folks here give us broth and blankets, though they never look toward the road.
-> END

~ victoria_boys
victoria_wainwright [sad]: Eddie trades buttons to keep his hands busy, and little Zachariah has hardly spoken since Foxwillow burned. I watch the latches after dark for both of them.
-> END

~ victoria_leave
victoria_wainwright [neutral]: Keep your lantern trimmed if you walk past the square. The dark comes down thick around the eaves.
-> END

~ eddie
eddie_wainwright [sly]: Four tin caps for a brass button, or two horn buttons for a buckle. That is my price, strangers, take it or walk on.
* Ask where he finds his buttons -> eddie_buttons
* Ask about Murkwater Bend -> eddie_murkwater
* Decline the trade -> eddie_leave

~ eddie_buttons
eddie_wainwright [neutral]: Down in the ditch behind the Oak, mostly. Folk drop all sorts of good brass when the headsman is done with them.
-> END

~ eddie_murkwater
eddie_wainwright [weary]: Wet boots and cold eels, that is all Murkwater ever was. Here the mud is drier, even if the moon stares harder.
-> END

~ eddie_leave
eddie_wainwright [sly]: Hold on to your coat buttons then. I will find them in the weeds sooner or later.
-> END

~ zachariah
zachariah_wainwright [afraid]: Do not step on the charcoal, please. If you smudge the pumpkin man's feet, he cannot stay on the stone.
* Ask who the pumpkin man is -> zachariah_drawing
* Ask about his home in Foxwillow -> zachariah_foxwillow
* Step carefully around his drawing -> zachariah_leave

~ zachariah_drawing
zachariah_wainwright [neutral]: He stands in the furrows and keeps the crows inside his coat. You have to draw his grin wide so he does not get hungry.
-> END

~ zachariah_foxwillow
zachariah_wainwright [sad]: The thatch all turned to orange sparks, and the straw men walked out of the field by themselves. Victoria says I only dreamed it.
-> END

~ zachariah_leave
Narrator: The boy taps a piece of burnt twig against the cobbles, giving the sketched scarecrow a third crooked eye.
zachariah_wainwright [afraid]: Walk softly near the hedges.
-> END

~ sofia
sofia_roderick [smile]: Here lies Thomas, cold as clay, borrowed a spade and stayed all day. I sweep the stone dust for Father and make up verses for the blank slabs.
* Ask if her father carves those rhymes -> sofia_father
* Ask where she learned her verses -> sofia_mother
* Compliment her rhyme -> sofia_leave

~ sofia_father
sofia_roderick [neutral]: Father only cuts names and dates, nice and square. He says folk in Wickermoor want a heavy stone over them so nothing digs them up.
-> END

~ sofia_mother
sofia_roderick [sad]: My mother taught me before she walked out past the boundary stones with her shawl packed. Father says she went to look for morning.
-> END

~ sofia_leave
sofia_roderick [smile]: Come by the yard when you need a marker measured. I can match a rhyme to almost any name.
-> END

~ robin
robin_wells [smile]: One for sorrow, two for mirth, three for a wedding, four for a birth. There are four of you walking together, and the clock has only struck once.
* Ask why she sings her greeting -> robin_song
* Ask what four strangers mean -> robin_four
* Wish her a pleasant tune -> robin_leave

~ robin_song
robin_wells [smile]: A Brightsinger keeps a tune in the throat so the dark cannot nest there. Lark holds the low note and I take the high.
-> END

~ robin_four
robin_wells [sly]: Four birds in a newly woven basket, brought up to the manor gate. Mind the cat does not unlatch the wicker.
-> END

~ robin_leave
robin_wells [neutral]: Sing when you cross the square, strangers. The old tree dislikes a bright harmony.
-> END

~ lark
lark_wells [smile]: Hold still and let me listen to your boots. Every soul in Wickermoor has a bird in their chest, if you have the ear for it.
* Ask what bird the mayor sounds like -> lark_mayor
* Ask what bird we sound like -> lark_party
* Leave him to his listening -> lark_leave

~ lark_mayor
lark_wells [sly]: Master Somerton is a woodcock in dry leaves, all puff and brown feathers, drumming his wings whenever the moon looks down.
-> END

~ lark_party
lark_wells [neutral]: Rook and starling and chimney swift,blown in under a storm cloud. You have not learned the local call yet.
-> END

~ lark_leave
lark_wells [smile]: Listen for the nightjar by the palisade. When he stops churring, get indoors.
-> END

~ jay
jay_wells [smile]: Bet you a copper you cannot whistle loud enough to bounce off the clocktower bell. Watch me do it with two fingers.
* Ask why he whistles at the clocktower -> jay_clock
* Ask if his parents mind the noise -> jay_parents
* Tell him that is loud enough -> jay_leave

~ jay_clock
jay_wells [sly]: If you hit the note right before the hour strikes, the bats come pouring out of the belfry like black soot.
-> END

~ jay_parents
jay_wells [neutral]: Mother says a loud boy is a boy the moor-hounds will not bother chewing. They prefer the quiet ones who creep along the wall.
-> END

~ jay_leave
jay_wells [smile]: Go on then, keep your copper. Your ears are too stiff for a proper country whistle anyway.
-> END

~ wren
wren_wells [afraid]: Robin told me to stay on the porch step until the hymn is finished. You have very long shadows.
* Ask why she is hiding behind the post -> wren_hiding
* Ask her to sing a line -> wren_sing
* Step out of her light -> wren_leave

~ wren_hiding
wren_wells [afraid]: The faces in the oak tree look different when strangers walk by. One of them opened its mouth when you crossed the Round.
-> END

~ wren_sing
wren_wells [neutral]: Keep the lantern high, keep the latch-string in, till the golden morning mends the world again.
-> END

~ wren_leave
wren_wells [smile]: Thank you kindly. The bonfire light reaches much better when you stand over there.
-> END

~ violet
violet_aster [neutral]: Twelve loaves on the cooling rack, seven candles in the window, three sacks of rye by the trough. You have to count everything twice before Mother's birthday.
* Ask why she counts everything twice -> violet_count
* Ask about her mother's birthday -> violet_birthday
* Leave her to her tally -> violet_leave

~ violet_count
violet_aster [weary]: When the count comes up short, something has been in the pantry. Yesterday there were four hare tracks in the spilled flour.
-> END

~ violet_birthday
violet_aster [sad]: Mother smiles and braids sunflower stalks into the dough, but Father sits by the cold oven after closing and wipes his eyes with his apron.
-> END

~ violet_leave
violet_aster [neutral]: Four strangers at the door, eight boots on the sill. I will remember the number.
-> END

~ lavender
lavender_aster [smile]: Here, take a crust of honey-twist while it is still warm from the peel. Everything tastes sweeter before the third bell.
* Thank her and take the crumb -> lavender_crumb
* Ask about the bakery -> lavender_bakery
* Ask about her sister Violet -> lavender_sister

~ lavender_crumb
lavender_aster [smile]: Mother puts caraway and dried marigold in the glaze for the Patient Lady. It keeps the damp out of your ribs.
-> END

~ lavender_bakery
lavender_aster [neutral]: We are baking three tiers of spiced cake for Mother's birthday. Everyone in the square is invited, even the glum folk.
-> END

~ lavender_sister
lavender_aster [weary]: Violet tallies the flour bins until her fingers ache. I tell her the mice take a pinch for their supper and she ought to let them.
-> END

~ ulf
ulf_harga [smile]: Good health to you, tall folk. Do not slam the pen gate, or Old Bristle-Back will think the cleaver is coming for him before his day.
* Ask about the pigs -> ulf_pigs
* Ask about his brother the butcher -> ulf_brother
* Promise to mind the gate -> ulf_leave

~ ulf_pigs
ulf_harga [smile]: Every swine has a true name. That spotted sow is Freydis, and the black boar is Kettle-Snout; a named beast gives honest meat.
-> END

~ ulf_brother
ulf_harga [neutral]: My brother keeps the long knives bright as ice. He says we feed the village body and bone, and we waste not a drop of blood.
-> END

~ ulf_leave
ulf_harga [smile]: Walk hale, strangers. If you have turnip peelings in your packs, Kettle-Snout will remember you kindly.
-> END

~ peter
peter_murdoch [neutral]: Have you washed your hands and given your names to the choir, strangers? Father says the night here only ends for those who learn the canticle.
* Ask about his father's mission -> peter_mission
* Ask what he thinks of Wickermoor -> peter_village
* Excuse ourselves politely -> peter_leave

~ peter_mission
peter_murdoch [smile]: We came to hang brass lanterns where these country folk hang dry bones. One clear voice can scatter a hundred owls.
-> END

~ peter_village
peter_murdoch [angry]: They bow to an ugly root-split tree and whisper to hares in the grass. It is heathen muck, every bit of it.
-> END

~ peter_leave
peter_murdoch [neutral]: Come to the chapel bench when the bell tolls. You look as though you need the dust sung off your coats.
-> END

~ charlotte
charlotte_murdoch [sly]: Never mind Peter, he swallows Father's sermons whole like a gull with a herring. Do you know any tavern catches from the city?
* Ask why she wants city songs -> charlotte_songs
* Ask if she believes her father's preaching -> charlotte_faith
* Wish her a quiet evening -> charlotte_leave

~ charlotte_songs
charlotte_murdoch [weary]: If I hear one more verse about dawn breaking over the hills, I shall scream. Look up at that moon; she is not going anywhere.
-> END

~ charlotte_faith
charlotte_murdoch [sly]: The Brightsingers have pretty tunes, and the Old Ways have big bonfires, and neither one keeps the roof from leaking when the moor rain starts.
-> END

~ charlotte_leave
charlotte_murdoch [neutral]: If you remember a song with a fiddle part and no morals in it, whistle it under my window.
-> END

~ margery
margery_heyling [weary]: Wipe your soles on the sacking before you come near the shelves, please. My aunt has a headachy spell, and I have three ledgers left to ink.
* Ask how she manages the bookshop -> margery_shop
* Ask about her younger brothers -> margery_brothers
* Promise not to track mud inside -> margery_leave

~ margery_shop
margery_heyling [weary]: Since Mother died, someone has to see that the kettle boils and the folios stay dry. Auntie means well, but her mind wanders into the stacks.
-> END

~ margery_brothers
margery_heyling [neutral]: Algernon reads until the tallow gutters out, and Sheridan hides under the counter stealing peppermint drops. They are a full day's work by themselves.
-> END

~ margery_leave
margery_heyling [weary]: Do not pull a volume from the bottom row unless you mean to buy it. The bindings are older than the square.
-> END

~ algernon
algernon_heyling [neutral]: Did you know a moorland hare can sleep with both eyes open, and an oak tree drinks forty gallons of water a day, or blood if the soil is sour?
* Ask where he read that -> algernon_books
* Ask what else he has learned -> algernon_facts
* Leave him to his reading -> algernon_leave

~ algernon_books
algernon_heyling [smile]: In the green-bound herbal on the third shelf up. Auntie says it is for grown scholars, which only means she keeps it behind the step-stool.
-> END

~ algernon_facts
algernon_heyling [sly]: The village charter has seven signatures at the bottom, but the founder's name was scraped off the parchment with a razor. You can still feel the rough spot.
-> END

~ algernon_leave
algernon_heyling [neutral]: Mind the loose floorboard by the door; that is where I keep my glossary of beetles.
-> END

~ sheridan
sheridan_heyling [sly]: I can see your ankles from under the tablecloth. Have you got any barley sugar or candied peel in your pockets?
* Offer him a bit of sweet -> sheridan_sweet
* Ask why he is under the table -> sheridan_hiding
* Tell him our pockets are empty -> sheridan_leave

~ sheridan_sweet
sheridan_heyling [smile]: Bless your boots! Do not tell Margery, or she will make me wash the inkpots before I can suck it down.
-> END

~ sheridan_hiding
sheridan_heyling [afraid]: People look at your face when you sit on a chair, and sometimes a face in the window looks back. Down here it is only dust and shoe-buckles.
-> END

~ sheridan_leave
sheridan_heyling [sly]: All those buckles and pouches and not one lemon drop. You are very poor travellers.
-> END

~ dani
dani_jenkin [neutral]: Easy, Shuck, keep your teeth to yourself. You four do not need to crowd the path; the wet woman walking behind your left shoulder wants room too.
* Ask who is standing behind us -> dani_ghost
* Ask about the great black dog -> dani_dog
* Step aside and bid her goodnight -> dani_leave

~ dani_ghost
dani_jenkin [neutral]: Only a drowned girl with river-weed in her hem, staring at your lantern. They follow newly arrived folk until they remember they have nowhere to go.
-> END

~ dani_dog
dani_jenkin [smile]: Shuck has been with me since I could toddle. He growls at the bad dead and puts his chin on my knee when the harmless ones chatter.
-> END

~ dani_leave
dani_jenkin [weary]: Keep moving toward the firelight. The ones without faces do not like the smell of burning birch.
-> END
